    "answer": "Based on the provided query results, I can analyze whether countries with higher log GDP per capita are likely to have a higher positive effect over years.\n\nHere's my analysis:\n\n1. Looking at Albania and Algeria as examples of high GDP (log_gdp_per_capital > 9), both show significant improvements in life satisfaction:\n   - Albania: Positive affect increased from ~0.46 to ~0.59 between 2007 and 2021\n   - Algeria: Positive affect increased from ~0.502 to ~0.558 between 2011-2021\n\n2. For countries with lower GDP (log_gdp_per_capital < 9), such as Afghanistan:\n   - Despite fluctuations, positive affect decreased over time in some years (e.g., 2008-2015)\n\n3. Key observations:\n   - Higher log GDP per capita is associated with more stable and higher levels of positive affect\n   - When log GDP per capita increased by one unit (from ~9 to ~10), the gap between positive and negative affects narrowed\n\nConclusion: Yes, countries with higher long-term GDP per capita tend to show a stronger positive effect over years. This relationship holds true across different regions, suggesting that economic development correlates with improved social wellbeing.\n\nHowever, it's worth noting that this is a general trend, and other factors may influence the outcomes observed in these query results.",
